Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP/RTU client for reading/writing PLC/field device registers
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns response objects with .isError()/.registers; pyModbusTCP's ModbusClient.read_holding_registers returns a plain list or 
    │   None on failure, requiring different error handling; modbus-tk uses a Master object with execute() and Modbus function codes rather than named 
    │   read/write methods; minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial/RTU communication (single-register read/write via Instrument class), not native TCP 
    │   master, and lacks a direct 'unit' keyword equivalent (uses slave address in constructor); Connection setup differs: pymodbus needs explicit .connect(),
    │   pyModbusTCP auto-connects or needs .open(), modbus-tk requires explicit serial/TCP transport construction; Register addressing/offset conventions can 
    │   differ slightly between libraries (0-based vs 1-based in some contexts)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages provide Modbus master/client functionality to read and write holding registers over TCP or serial, which is the core operation 
        performed here (read_holding_registers, write_register). They are functionally interchangeable at the protocol level, but each has a distinct client 
        object model, error-handling convention, and connection setup, so a thin adapter/connector is needed to preserve the current interface (isError() 
        checks, unit= keyword, register list return type).

